class:: CQCC
summary:: Constant-Q cepstra after uniform-frequency resampling
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/MFCC2

description::
Computes a constant-Q magnitude spectrum, squares it, applies a natural logarithm with a power floor, interpolates that log-power curve at uniform Hz positions, then applies an orthonormal DCT-II. Interpolation weights are linear in Hz between adjacent logarithmically spaced analysis frequencies. Uniform positions include fmin and the highest CQT centre. The DCT uses cos(pi*k*(i+0.5)/uniformBins), scaled by sqrt(1/uniformBins) for k=0 and sqrt(2/uniformBins) otherwise.

The processing sequence follows Todisco, Delgado and Evans, A New Feature for Automatic Speaker Verification Anti-Spoofing: Constant Q Cepstral Coefficients (Odyssey 2016), link::https://www.isca-archive.org/odyssey_2016/todisco16_odyssey.pdf::. MIR2 uses piecewise linear interpolation of log power; the published CQCC system uses a spline resampling method. This choice is explicit and does not claim reference-system numerical equivalence. There is no automatic mean normalization, liftering, or delta concatenation; use the separate feature dynamics utilities when needed.

Each analysis band uses a separate variable-length Hann-windowed complex sinusoid. With B bins per octave, band k is centred at fmin * 2.pow(k/B), and its kernel length is ceil(sampleRate / ((2.pow(1/B)-1)*frequency + gamma)). CQT uses gamma=0. The kernels are centred inside a common power-of-two frame and transformed to sparse spectral rows at construction. Processing performs one FFT and complex dot products with those rows. These are variable-length analysis filters, rather than interpolated FFT magnitudes.

The independent implementation follows Judith Brown, Calculation of a Constant Q Spectral Transform (1991), and Brown and Puckette, An Efficient Algorithm for the Calculation of a Constant Q Transform (1992). Sources: link::https://www.media.mit.edu/publications/calculation-of-a-constant-q-spectral-transform/:: and link::https://www.media.mit.edu/publications/an-efficient-algorithm-for-the-calculation-of-a-constant-q-transform/::.

The longest kernel sets a common FFT frame N, limited to 32768 samples. The first result follows N input samples and refers to approximately the frame centre, N/2 samples in the past; outputs hold between hops. The highest kernel must keep its Hann main lobe below Nyquist. Invalid frequency ranges, an oversized frame, insufficient real-time memory, or more than 262144 stored spectral coefficients disable the unit with an error message. Memory and all kernel transforms are prepared at construction; callbacks do not allocate. Extremely low frequencies or many bins per octave require a longer frame, and increasing gamma in VQT trades frequency resolution for shorter latency.

The sparsity threshold discards small kernel spectrum coefficients relative to each row's maximum. This bounds storage by a fixed budget but is an approximation; it is not a guaranteed relative error on every signal. No external transform library is linked. Normal input is unscaled; nonfinite samples become zero and extreme values are limited to +/-1e6 for numerical safety.

Before the first analysis all coefficients are zero. After analysis, silence produces sqrt(uniformBins)*log(logFloor) at coefficient zero and approximately zero at higher coefficients. Omitting coefficient zero avoids the constant floor term. The CQT cost is shared within this unit; resampling costs O(uniformBins) and the DCT O(numCoeffs*uniformBins) each hop.

Vanilla MFCC computes cepstra from a Mel filterbank. CQCC begins with variable-duration constant-Q filters and resamples in linear frequency before the cepstral transform, giving a different frequency weighting and latency.

classmethods::
method:: kr

argument:: in
Audio-rate source, with multichannel expansion supported.

argument:: numCoeffs
Literal number of cepstral outputs, 1..64, default 13. Including the omitted zeroth order, if any, it must not exceed uniformBins.

argument:: fmin
Positive initialization-time centre frequency of the first bin, in Hz; default 55.

argument:: numBins
Literal number of output analysis bands from 2 through 192; default 84.

argument:: binsPerOctave
Literal frequency grid density from 6 through 48; default 12.

argument:: hop
Literal fraction of the automatically chosen common frame, 0.0625..1; default 0.25.

argument:: uniformBins
Literal number of uniformly spaced Hz samples for resampling, 16..1024, default 256. More samples increase DCT work without creating new spectral information.

argument:: include0
Literal 1 to include coefficient zero, or 0 to begin at coefficient one. Default 1.

argument:: logFloor
Positive power floor before natural logarithms; values below 1e-30 are clipped.

argument:: sparsity
Literal kernel spectrum cutoff relative to each kernel maximum, 1e-8..0.01; default 1e-4. Lower values retain more coefficients and can exceed the storage budget.

returns::
Array of numCoeffs cepstral values. These are signed log-power coordinates, not bounded probabilities.

examples::
Two nonconstant cepstral coordinates control filter colour and stereo position, making the changing spectral-envelope description audible.
code::
(
Routine({
    ~mir2Buffer = Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.child);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1);
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);
        var coefficients = CQCC.kr(source, numCoeffs: 8, fmin: 110, numBins: 48, include0: 0);
        var shape = (coefficients[0] * 0.08).tanh.lag(0.05);
        var detail = (coefficients[1] * 0.08).tanh.lag(0.05);
        var filtered = RLPF.ar(source, shape.linexp(-1, 1, 250, 4500), 0.3) * 0.25;
        var sound = Pan2.ar(filtered, detail * 0.8);
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.35)
    }.play;
}).play(AppClock);
)
::
Stop and release the buffer:
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
~mir2Buffer.free;
)
::

